  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/parosphromenus-phoenicurus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Family: Osphronemidae</li><li>Temperament: peaceful, but males can be territorial</li><li>Size: males up to 4 cm, females slightly smaller</li><li>TDS: 0-60</li><li>Temperature: 23-28 °C (74-82 °F)</li><li>pH: 4-6 (low pH water is recommended)</li><li>Difficulty: expert</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/parosphromenus-phoenicurus",
      "html": "<p>Expert level: these fish are not for beginners as they only eat live food. Be sure to have the habitat ready before purchase.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/parosphromenus-phoenicurus",
      "html": "<p>Found only in the blackwater swamps near Kota Kerincikiri, on the Indonesian island of Sumatra.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/parosphromenus-phoenicurus",
      "html": "<p>In the wild, it is found in slow-moving and shaded water bodies with a pH between 4.0 and 6.0 and a temperature range of 22 to 28 °C (71.6 to 82.4 °F).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/parosphromenus-phoenicurus",
      "html": "<p>They are small fish, so it is recommended to keep them in groups of 5 in a 5 gallon (about 19 litre) tank or larger unless breeding. In that case, they are best kept in pairs.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/parosphromenus-phoenicurus",
      "html": "<p>TDS 0-60; temperature 23-28 °C (74-82 °F); pH 4-6 (water with low pH is recommended).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/parosphromenus-phoenicurus",
      "html": "<p>They feed on small aquatic invertebrates such as insect larvae and crustaceans. In the aquarium: blackworms, brine shrimp and daphnia (they only eat live food).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/parosphromenus-phoenicurus",
      "html": "<p>Peaceful, but males can be territorial.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/parosphromenus-phoenicurus",
      "html": "<p>Males have much more colourful and elongated fins. Adult males reach about 4 cm, while females are slightly smaller.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/parosphromenus-phoenicurus",
      "html": "<p>Compared to other Licorice Gouramis, it can be told apart from the similar-looking Bintan or Harveyi type by the substantial black and red parallel bands on the unpaired fins. The body is elongated and laterally compressed, dark brown to blackish on the back and lighter brown on the sides. The most striking feature is the bright red fins, including the dorsal, anal and caudal fins.</p>"
    }
  ],
